*.py text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
# The migration ledger identifies each migration by its bytes, and the deploy
# box compares the digest it has on record against the one it reads here. Grading
# them exactly as they lay was how one committed LF file, materialised CRLF by
# this checkout's `core.autocrlf=true`, came to carry two digests and read as
# `APPLIED, THEN EDITED` on the box for a file nobody had touched — measured for
# `037` and again for `064_seb_door_refusals.sql`. The ledger folds CRLF to LF
# before hashing now (`canonical_bytes`), so the digest no longer depends on the
# platform; the *file* still does, and 27 of the 73 migrations in
# `supabase/migrations` were materialised CRLF before this line existed. Pinned
# like every other extension above, for the same reason: one file, one identity,
# on every platform that applies it. `tests/unit/test_apply_migration.py` fails
# if an extension this tool hashes is ever left unpinned the way this one was.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
# The git hooks carry no extension, so `*.sh` never matched them. Left to
# core.autocrlf they are checked out as CRLF on Windows, and a hook whose first
# line ends with a carriage return is not a shebang — it fails with "bad
# interpreter" on the Linux box that runs it.
deploy/git-hooks/* text eol=lf
